\title{The $L^2$ Energy Characterisation of Sidon Sets:\\ A Two-Kernel Decomposition}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A finite set of integers is a \emph{Sidon set} (equivalently a $B_2$ set) if
all of its pairwise sums are distinct up to the trivial symmetry. We give a
complete and self-contained treatment of Sidon sets from the point of view of
\emph{additive energy}---the number of quadruples $(a,b,c,d)$ from the set with
$a+b=c+d$---which equals the squared $L^2$ norm of the set's self-convolution
kernel. We prove two results. First, a universal lower bound: every finite set
$s$ of integers satisfies $\En[s] \ge 2|s|^2 - |s|$. Second, an exact
characterisation: $s$ is a Sidon set if and only if it attains this floor,
$\En[s] = 2|s|^2 - |s|$. Both follow from a single structural fact: the set of
energy quadruples always contains, and for Sidon sets exactly equals, the
almost-disjoint union of two elementary convolution kernels---a diagonal kernel
and a coordinate-swap kernel, each a shifted copy of $s \times s$, overlapping
in exactly $|s|$ elements. Sidon sets are thus the exact $L^2$-energy
minimisers, and the ``multi-kernel'' structure behind that minimisation is a
rigid two-element skeleton, universal across all sets. We include worked
examples, algorithms, and quantitative and higher-order extensions.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Let $s$ be a finite set of integers. Its \emph{sumset} is the set of all values
$a+b$ with $a,b \in s$. A classical question, going back to Simon Sidon's work
in the 1930s on lacunary Fourier series, asks how additively ``spread out'' $s$
can be. The extremal objects are the \emph{Sidon sets}: those in which
$a+b=c+d$ has only trivial solutions.

Sidon sets recur across mathematics under many names. As \emph{Golomb rulers},
they describe tick placements in which every pair of marks is a distinct
distance apart, prized in radar and sonar array design. In coding theory and in
frequency-hopping communication they minimise interference. In number theory
they are the sets whose representation function $r_s(x) = \#\{(a,b) \in s^2 :
a+b=x\}$ is as flat as possible, saturating the density bound $|s| \lesssim
\sqrt{N}$ for subsets of $\{1,\dots,N\}$.

This paper isolates the $L^2$ (convolution-energy) face of the theory. We study
the additive energy $\En[s]$ and prove that the Sidon property is equivalent to
$\En[s]$ attaining a universal floor. The argument is elementary and
structural: it exhibits the energy as governed by exactly two convolution
kernels.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Universal energy floor (Theorem~\ref{thm:floor}): $\En[s] \ge 2|s|^2 -
  |s|$ for every finite $s \subset \Z$.
  \item Sidon $\Leftrightarrow$ minimal energy (Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}): $s$ is
  Sidon iff $\En[s] = 2|s|^2 - |s|$.
  \item Two-kernel decomposition (Section~\ref{sec:kernels}): the structural
  engine behind both results, with certifying kernel family of cardinality
  exactly two, independent of $|s|$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $s$ denotes a finite subset of $\Z$ and $|s|$ its cardinality;
quadruples are ordered.

\begin{definition}[Sidon set]
A finite set $s \subset \Z$ is a \emph{Sidon set} (a $B_2$ set) if for all
$a,b,c,d \in s$,
\[
  a + b = c + d \implies (a = c \ \text{or}\ a = d).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Representation function / self-convolution]
For $x \in \Z$ let $r_s(x) = \#\{(a,b) \in s \times s : a + b = x\}$. The
function $r_s = \mathbf{1}_s * \mathbf{1}_s$ is the \emph{self-convolution
kernel} of $s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Additive energy]
The \emph{additive energy} of $s$ is
$\En[s] = \#\{(a,b,c,d) \in s^4 : a+b = c+d\}.$
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[$L^2$ identity]\label{prop:parseval}
$\displaystyle \En[s] = \sum_{x \in \Z} r_s(x)^2.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Group quadruples with $a+b=c+d$ by their common value $x=a+b=c+d$. For fixed
$x$ there are $r_s(x)$ choices of $(a,b)$ and independently $r_s(x)$ choices of
$(c,d)$, giving $r_s(x)^2$ quadruples; sum over $x$.
\end{proof}

Thus additive energy is the squared $L^2$ norm of the convolution kernel, and
minimising energy is minimising that $L^2$ mass. Equivalently, writing
$\widehat{\mathbf{1}_s}(\theta) = \sum_{a\in s} e^{2\pi i a\theta}$, Parseval
gives $\En[s] = \int_0^1 |\widehat{\mathbf{1}_s}(\theta)|^4\,d\theta$, so
additive energy is the fourth moment of the exponential sum; the results below
identify its exact minimisers as the Sidon sets.

\begin{definition}[Energy quadruple set]
Encoding a quadruple $(a,b,c,d)$ as the pair-of-pairs $((a,c),(b,d))$, the
\emph{energy set} of $s$ is
\[
  \Ec(s) = \{((a,c),(b,d)) \in (s\times s)\times(s\times s) : a+b = c+d\}.
\]
By construction $|\Ec(s)| = \En[s]$.
\end{definition}

\section{The two-kernel decomposition}\label{sec:kernels}

\begin{definition}[Diagonal kernel]
$\Ac(s) = \{((a,a),(b,b)) : (a,b) \in s \times s\}$, the identities $a+b=a+b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Swap kernel]
$\Bc(s) = \{((a,b),(b,a)) : (a,b) \in s \times s\}$, the identities $a+b=b+a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Kernel cardinalities]\label{lem:card}
Each coordinate map above is injective on $s\times s$, so
$|\Ac(s)| = |\Bc(s)| = |s|^2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $((a,a),(b,b))$ one recovers $(a,b)$; from $((a,b),(b,a))$ one recovers
$(a,b)$ from the first pair. Injective images preserve cardinality and $|s\times
s| = |s|^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Overlap]\label{lem:overlap}
$\Ac(s) \cap \Bc(s) = \{((a,a),(a,a)) : a \in s\}$, of cardinality $|s|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$((a,a),(b,b)) \in \Bc(s)$ iff it has the form $((p,q),(q,p))$; matching
coordinates forces $p=q=a=b$. Conversely each such element is the image of $a$
under the injection $a \mapsto ((a,a),(a,a))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Union count]\label{lem:union}
$|\Ac(s) \cup \Bc(s)| + |s| = 2|s|^2$, i.e.\ $|\Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)| = 2|s|^2-|s|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Inclusion--exclusion with Lemmas~\ref{lem:card} and \ref{lem:overlap}:
$|\Ac\cup\Bc| = |s|^2 + |s|^2 - |s|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Kernels are genuine energy quadruples]\label{lem:sub}
$\Ac(s) \cup \Bc(s) \subseteq \Ec(s)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$((a,a),(b,b))$ decodes to $(a,b,a,b)$ with $a+b=a+b$; $((a,b),(b,a))$ decodes
to $(a,b,b,a)$ with $a+b=b+a$. Both are in $\Ec(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Rigidity for Sidon sets]\label{lem:rigid}
If $s$ is Sidon then $\Ec(s) \subseteq \Ac(s) \cup \Bc(s)$, hence
$\Ec(s) = \Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $((a,c),(b,d)) \in \Ec(s)$, so $a+b=c+d$ with all in $s$. By the Sidon
property $a=c$ or $a=d$. If $a=c$ then $b=d$, giving $((a,a),(b,b))\in\Ac(s)$.
If $a=d$ then $b=c$, giving $((a,b),(b,a))\in\Bc(s)$. Combine with
Lemma~\ref{lem:sub}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Converse rigidity]\label{lem:conv}
If $\Ec(s) \subseteq \Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)$ then $s$ is Sidon.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $a+b=c+d$ with all in $s$, then $((a,c),(b,d)) \in \Ec(s)$ lies in $\Ac(s)$
(so $a=c$) or in $\Bc(s)$ (so $a=d$). Either way the Sidon condition holds.
\end{proof}

\section{Main results}

\begin{theorem}[Universal energy floor]\label{thm:floor}
For every finite $s \subset \Z$, $\;2|s|^2 \le \En[s] + |s|$, i.e.\ $\En[s] \ge
2|s|^2 - |s|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\En[s] = |\Ec(s)| \ge |\Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)| = 2|s|^2 - |s|$ by
Lemmas~\ref{lem:sub} and \ref{lem:union}.
\end{proof}

In convolution terms (Proposition~\ref{prop:parseval}), $\sum_x r_s(x)^2 \ge
2|s|^2 - |s|$.

\begin{theorem}[Sidon $\Leftrightarrow$ minimal energy]\label{thm:iff}
A finite set $s \subset \Z$ is Sidon iff $\En[s] + |s| = 2|s|^2$, i.e.\
$\En[s] = 2|s|^2 - |s|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) By Lemma~\ref{lem:rigid}, $\En[s] = |\Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)| =
2|s|^2-|s|$.
($\Leftarrow$) If $\En[s] = 2|s|^2-|s|$ then $|\Ec(s)| = |\Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)|$;
since $\Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s) \subseteq \Ec(s)$ and the cardinalities agree, the sets
are equal, so $\Ec(s) \subseteq \Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:conv}
applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact minimisers]
Among all subsets of $\Z$ of fixed cardinality $n$, the minimum additive energy
is $2n^2-n$, attained by exactly the Sidon sets of that size.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Energy defect]\label{cor:defect}
For finite $s$, the quantity $D(s) := \En[s] - (2|s|^2-|s|) \ge 0$ vanishes iff
$s$ is Sidon, and equals the number of energy quadruples of $s$ outside
$\Ac(s)\cup\Bc(s)$, i.e.\ its non-trivial additive coincidences.
\end{corollary}

\section{Worked examples}

Write $\mathrm{floor}(n)=2n^2-n$.

\begin{example}[$\{0,1,3,7\}$, Sidon]
$n=4$, $\mathrm{floor}=28$, and $\En=28$, so $D=0$.
\end{example}
\begin{example}[$\{0,1,2,3\}$, non-Sidon]
$n=4$, $\mathrm{floor}=28$, but $\En=44$, so $D=16$ (e.g.\ $0+2=1+1$,
$0+3=1+2$, $1+3=2+2$ and orderings).
\end{example}
\begin{example}[$\{0,1,2\}$]
$n=3$, $\mathrm{floor}=15$, $\En=19$, $D=4$.
\end{example}
\begin{example}[$\{0,1,3\}$, Sidon]
$n=3$, $\mathrm{floor}=15$, $\En=15$, $D=0$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Additive energy by convolution.} Tally the $n^2$ pairwise sums into
a dictionary to obtain $r_s$, then return $\sum_x r_s(x)^2$; $O(n^2)$ time and
space (Proposition~\ref{prop:parseval}).

\paragraph{Sidon test.} Either insert each $a+b$ ($a\le b$) into a set and
check for collisions, or compute $\En[s]$ and test $\En[s]=2n^2-n$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}); both $O(n^2)$.

\paragraph{Energy defect.} Return $D(s)=\En[s]-(2n^2-n)$
(Corollary~\ref{cor:defect}).

\section{Relation to the classical size bound}

The $L^2$ story dovetails with the classical size theory. If $s \subseteq
\{1,\dots,N\}$ is Sidon, all $\binom{|s|}{2}$ positive pairwise differences are
distinct in $\{1,\dots,N-1\}$, so $\binom{|s|}{2} \le N-1$ and $|s| \le
\sqrt{N}+O(1)$. Dually, among sets of fixed cardinality the Sidon sets are the
minimal-energy ones: maximal size for fixed range and minimal energy for fixed
cardinality are two faces of the same distinctness condition, with $2|s|^2-|s|$
the energy-side analogue of the square-root density bound.

\section{Applications and discussion}

In radar/sonar arrays (Golomb rulers), a Sidon layout makes every pairwise
spacing distinct, and the energy floor is a single-integer optimality
certificate. In communications, additive energy bounds worst-case interference,
minimised exactly by Sidon configurations. In additive combinatorics, the exact
identity $D(s) = \En[s]-(2n^2-n)$ turns ``low energy implies structure'' into an
exact census of coincidences.

The mechanism, not the value $2n^2-n$, is the point: the entire minimum is
witnessed by exactly two elementary convolution kernels, each an injective copy
of $s\times s$, overlapping in $|s|$ points. The skeleton is universal (same
maps for every $s$), minimal (one kernel is short of the floor, two suffice
exactly, a third cannot help for a Sidon set), and it reframes weighted
multi-kernel smoothing as perturbation of a fixed two-kernel core.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Two kernels are optimal, three are never needed.} We conjecture that
for a Sidon set the trivial additive coincidences decompose into precisely two
elementary convolution kernels, that no family of fewer than two reproduces the
exact minimum, and no family of more than two ever lowers it; the minimal exact
certifying family has cardinality exactly two, independent of $|s|$.

\paragraph{A quantitative energy defect controls near-Sidon sets.} If a set
misses the Sidon condition by $k$ non-trivial quadruples, its energy should
exceed the minimum by a linear function of $k$; conversely any set within $k$ of
the minimum should be reparable to Sidon by deleting $O(\sqrt{k})$ elements.
Corollary~\ref{cor:defect} gives the exact defect identity; the removal
statement with explicit constants remains open.

\paragraph{Higher-order Sidon sets and the two-to-$h!$ kernel jump.} For $B_h$
sets, we conjecture the $h$-fold self-convolution kernel is bounded pointwise
by $h!$, energy is minimised by exactly such sets, and the minimal exact kernel
family jumps from two ($h=2$) to $h!$, indexed by permutations of the $h$
summands.

\section{Conclusion}

Sidon sets are precisely the finite integer sets whose additive energy attains
the universal floor $2|s|^2-|s|$. Both floor and characterisation flow from a
rigid two-kernel decomposition of the energy quadruple set into a diagonal copy
and a swap copy of $s\times s$, delimiting the sense in which multi-kernel
smoothing has a fixed skeleton and opening quantitative and higher-order
refinements.

